  "image_name": "1c70a32ff59ed05cd580f7a8eb304c74.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes several ways programmable nucleases, especially CRISPR–Cas, can be used to manipulate DNA and cell populations. At the top, three editing platforms—ZFNs, TALENs, and CRISPR–Cas—are shown recognizing target DNA and creating cuts. Those breaks can then be repaired either by error-prone non-homologous end joining, producing small insertions or deletions, or by homology-directed repair using a template to install precise sequence changes.\n\nThe middle panels show CRISPR used without necessarily changing sequence. Binding near promoter elements appears to activate transcription by helping recruit RNA polymerase, or repress transcription by blocking either initiation or elongation. Other panels illustrate sequence-specific killing of chosen bacterial genotypes, leaving untargeted cells behind, and selective enrichment of recombinant or converted genomes from a mixed population.\n\nAt the bottom, the figure depicts a gene drive strategy. A nuclease targets the wild-type allele, and repair converts it into the engineered version, so the modified allele is copied onto the homologous chromosome. The inheritance diagram then shows this engineered allele spreading through successive generations at higher-than-Mendelian frequency. Overall, the figure emphasizes CRISPR’s versatility in editing, regulation, selection, and inheritance bias.",
